<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shamakhi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shemakha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shamakhi is a town in the mountains of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, for centuries the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a> and of the province of Shirvan. A centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade, it was sacked in 1721 by Lezgin rebels, an event that helped prompt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s Persian campaign. Repeated earthquakes damaged it, and after the quake of 1859 the provincial capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shamakhi.jpg" alt="Shamakhi" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sefer azeri · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montage_of_%C5%9Eamax%C4%B1_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty ruled from <b>Shamakhi</b>, also called Yazidiyya after the family, and gradually extended its authority over neighbouring districts, absorbing Layzan in the early tenth century and at times controlling the city of Derbent to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sunni Lezgins led by Haji Davud Bey took <b>Shamakhi</b>, the capital of the Safavid province of Shirvan, after the central government abandoned its counter-campaign at a critical moment.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Surkhay Khan I joined the rebel Hajji Davud in sacking <b>Shamakhi</b> in 1721, an event that helped trigger Peter the Great's Persian campaign, and briefly ruled Shirvan as an Ottoman vassal.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazikumukh-khanate">Kazikumukh Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Fatali Khan, who ruled from 1758, conquered the Derbent Khanate in 1765, dominated the Baku Khanate and the Javad Khanate and took <b>Shamakhi</b>, creating the largest state in the eastern Caucasus.</blockquote><a href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, local leaders in Shirvan formed an independent khanate around <b>Shamakhi</b>, the old capital of the Shirvanshah State.</blockquote><a href="/state/shirvan-khanate">Shirvan Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
